WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.886
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.080
NARRATOR: A commercial drone
is a small, unmanned aircraft

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.030
with an onboard camera.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.640
It can capture a bird's
eye view of the ground,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.340
collecting up to the minute
data useful for many types

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.870
of applications.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.420
They're used to plant
infrastructure projects,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.990
oversee natural resources,
and manage farmland.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.160
Spanning about five
feet in diameter,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.700
this commercial drone is
also known as an octo copter.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.190
It has eight rotors, which
enable it to maneuver

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.830
in the air like a helicopter.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.930
The drone's components
are made of carbon

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.920
fiber, a highly stiff,
strong, and durable material.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.080
Carbon fiber is
also lightweight,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.960
a critical factor in aviation.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.050
Computer guided milling
machine cuts to parts.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.620
A technician manually
sands their edges smooth.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.670
This component makes up half
of one of the drone's legs.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.040
The mill cuts out the
top and bottom frames

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.120
for the drone's body.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.440
Technicians then assemble
the two halves of each leg.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.120
Some of the drone's components
are produced by a 3D printer.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.190
The printer builds each
part layer by layer out

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.525
of a thin stream of plastic.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.710
This is one of the drone's
eight armed receptacles.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.580
A technician drills a hole
where the arm will be fitted.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.140
The hole must be precise
to ensure a secure fit.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.390
After verifying its diameter
with digital calipers,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.300
the technician installs the
back part of the receptacle,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.325
and the lever that clamps the
inserted arm firmly in place.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.270
He attaches an electronic
speed controller to one end.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.430
Then he screws the receptacle
to the bottom frame

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.540
of the drone's body.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.740
The wires coming out the
other end of the controller

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.190
connect to an electrical power
harness and to the autopilot.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.110
The autopilot is the onboard
computer that runs the drone.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:19.030
He installs the seven
remaining receptacles,

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.035
then the power harness.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.490
It has eight connectors, one
for each electronic speed

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.603
controller.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:38.390
Next, he attaches
the top frame, then

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.220
the payload mount that holds
the camera and hard drive.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.610
He links the payload mount
to a servo motor, which

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.200
is guided by the autopilot.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.000
The drone's body is
protected by a cover.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.120
Workers mount a sheet of plastic
on a vacuum former machine.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.660
They start up the machine's
heating element and position

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.100
the plastic just beneath it.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.500
This gradually heats the
plastic to a malleable state.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.320
Once the plastic is soft,
they position a wooden mold

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:21.620
on the machine, activate
the suction, then drop

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.690
the sheet onto the mold.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.460
The vacuum draws the
plastic tightly against it.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.740
They let the vacuum-formed
plastic cool and harden,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.645
then carefully trim
off the excess.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.930
They cut out a rough opening
for the payload mount.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.800
Through that opening
they secure the cover

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.500
to the computer guided
milling machine,

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.770
which finalizes the shape
to precise specifications.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.450
Once the cover is finished, they
install it on the top frame.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.750
They attach the four
legs to the bottom frame.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.110
Then they install the camera
onto the payload mount.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.810
The mount pivots to angle
the camera at the ground.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.270
They insert and clamp the
arms into the receptacles.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.660
Each arm is a carbon fiber
tube with a plastic rotor

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.360
that's driven by a
small motor underneath.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.740
Upon inserting an arm,
its motor makes contact

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.260
with its electronic
speed controller.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.080
They also install the autopilot.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.140
The autopilot executes
the programmed flight path

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.570
and operates the
camera, the servo motor

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.500
that moves the payload, and the
electronic speed controllers.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.990
The drone operator can
communicate wirelessly

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.930
with the autopilot
using a remote control,

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.330
but it's more common
to program the GPS

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.400
coordinates of the drone's
route prior to take off.

